    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the machine experiences an unexpected decline in value or if it breaks down earlier than anticipated, it may qualify for different kinds of depreciation accounting such as accelerated depreciation which includes methods like double-declining balance or sum-of-the-years'-digits, to better reflect the loss in value during the earlier part of its useful life.\n\nFor a mid-year change in depreciation due to reduced useful life or value, you would reevaluate your annual depreciation expense based on the new expected remaining useful life and salvage value. This would require a year-end adjustment to ensure your financial reporting accurately reflects the asset's current condition and value.\n\nIt's advisable to consult with an accountant or a financial advisor to get guidance on the proper accounting treatment and make sure to journal entries are accurately reported in your financial statements according to the Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) or International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS), depending on your jurisdiction.",
